“Christians, but not Christians”:

The use of pagan festivals by Christians as Christian festivals, such as Christmas, the Epiphany, and Good Friday;

The introduction of the Book of Revelation, as a literary device for the spiritual and political control of the faithful;

The introduction of the term “pagan” as an epithet of Christians, and the rejection of most of the ancient traditions and religious practices of the Christian Roman Empire;

The rejection of nearly all the ancient Christian practices and traditions of the pagan Roman Empire, such as baptism, the Eucharist, the Mass, the Holy Days of Obligation, Vespers, Matins, and Holy Thursday, to name a few.

The Church of Christ, the first church established in Jerusalem by Jesus Christ Himself, and the only church that Jesus Himself founded, continues to maintain the ancient Christian tradition of the Roman Empire, however.

